so as of today i'm in my second year of  my phds so this time i wanted to talk a  little bit into the amazing topic of our  gut and how our gut affects our brain to  this god's brain axis as they now call  it and probably if i didn't do my  research in computational neuroscience i  wouldn't have done it about the gut  because they nowadays call our gut our  second brain because that's how much  influence the gut has on our general  well-being and our mood and i kind of  want to talk about how we can optimize  our diet to improve our gut health and  through improving our gut health also  improving our brain function so i've  gone through all the papers and linked  all the resources down below so if you  have anything that you think like ah  that's maybe not entirely right or not  entirely what i'm looking for i do  encourage you highly to also look at  some papers yourself and find some of  the resources that you need for the  questions you have  so grab yourself a coffee  and let's have coffee time  [Music]  so in neuroscience we kind of had to  rediscover that our brain actually does  not exist in a vacuum but it actually  exists in our body so the health of our  body directly influences how our brain  functions and as our brain is kind of  the view of how we perceive the world  our body then also influences things  like our mood and our perception of the  world and one of the biggest influers  that determines our bodily health and  therefore our brain health is the gut so  the gut is one part of the body that has  a direct link to the brain through the  vagus nerve and we can kind of see the  vagus nerve as the highway in our body  where the fastest traveling signals go  through and this is then the link  between our two brains so on the one  hand the brain in your head and on the  other hand the brain in your gusto  intestinal tract and the main function  scientists thought primarily of this  direct link was to tell the brain  exactly when we are full or when we are  hungry and evolutionarily of course this  is the most important  thing for us to find food and survive so  another way that the guts in the brain  are actually linked is through  neurotransmitters and neurotransmitters  are the body's chemical messengers so  for example serotonin is one of these  messengers that helps stabilize our mood  regulates our happiness and gives  feelings of general  well-being  and one of the interesting things is  that serotonin isn't primarily produced  in the brain but actually is also  produced in our guts which is then quite  interesting so if you eat ice cream and  you get this feeling of intense  happiness this intense happiness feeling  probably doesn't directly originate from  your brain but actually originates  directly from your stomach  but nowadays scientists are also  figuring out that not only feelings of  extreme happiness can originate from  your gut but also  more  negative feelings such as anxiousness or  depression so the saying a gut feeling  is actually less subjective than one  might think so let's get into a little  bit of the research  so there are two type of main research  done in this area of gut health and its  correlation to the brain and one is done  on animals primarily rats which actually  have very similar brains to humans and  the other research is done directly on  human participants  but the negative point of  research with people like you and me is  that we cannot just  influence all the factors so i want you  to keep this in the back of your mind  whilst i present the research  so let's first start with the research  on animals  so our really interesting research  showed that if you had certain mice that  were more anxious as opposed to more  happy mice that if you transferred their  gut microbes the anxious mice actually  became more happy or less anxious  and  non-anxious mice or the normal mice and  she became more anxious and this is  really interesting because this shows  that there is a direct causal link  between the bacteria that live in our  gut and the feelings that animals have  and we are also animals so probably for  us this is also true so if you're  feeling extremely upset or anxious it's  maybe not only coming from your brain  but maybe also from what you just ate or  if you have an upset stomach so that's  something to think about and it has also  been shown that signals from our guts  directly activate the hippocampus and  the hippocampus is this center mainly  known for learning and memory and  scientists used to think that this was  then  activated for animals as well as humans  to remember where they found the food  and that's why probably when you think  back on this loving date you had that  you can remember the spaghetti you ate  much more clearly than the conversation  you actually had and i was also shown by  a study on mice that when they had a  beef and rich diet versus a normal diet  that their learning and memory was  actually improved a little bit and  that's not to say that you now have to  go eat a lot of beef but it is to say  that if you change the gut microbe  learning and memory is directly affected  and another extremely interesting  finding in animals was that if they  changed or transferred certain gut  microbes from one mice to another that  actually their behavior could change so  their personality could actually change  depending on what kind of bacteria they  had in their gut  so this is kind of to say that if you  have a really grumpy neighbor maybe it's  not that he has a bad personality maybe  his gut is just upset so next time he  screams at you about some trash you  didn't throw away properly just tell him  to eat a yogurt and perhaps your  relationship problems will be solved so  now let's go away from the animals let's  go into some human research  so one of the interesting findings in  human research was that people who have  feelings of depression anxiety and  correlations of these that they also  haven't  disturbed a gut microbe and that's and  it is kind of hard to pinpoint exactly  if the feelings of depression or anxiety  cause the stomach to be upset or if the  upset stomach causes feelings of  depression or anxiety and furthermore it  has also been shown that the gut's  microbiota  and our diet actually also  influences the course of certain  psychiatric disorders  so there are now some doctors that  propose a more holistic view of the  treatment of these psychiatric disorders  and this is to say that they don't only  propose medicine as a cure but medicine  together with some psychological  interviews as well as an exercise regime  and probably re-learning how to have a  healthy lifestyle which then also  includes diet and another study has  actually shown that the transfer of  lean individuals got microbiota to  more heavier individuals actually  increase the insulin sensitivity of  these heavier individuals and insulin  sensitivity is one of the pre-diabetic  conditions so this is to say that  actually some of the cures of obesity  could perhaps be found in having a more  healthy god's microbiome and this could  then  for example be introduced by a healthier  diet or by directly transferring these  guts microbiota between individuals  which i think is super interesting  because this really shows  that the disbalance of wanting to eat a  lot more for people with a heavier  weight doesn't only come from the mind  but actually it's also their body  telling them dispersing signals this is  then one of the first indication that  good bacteria can help with maintaining  unhealthy weight and bad bacteria can  actually increase some of the weight  gain so let's get into some tips and  tricks to actually solve some of the  problems that you may have with your  guts  so i do want to say i'm not a dietitian  so if you're thinking about introducing  any of the things i say into your diet  and you have some doubts if it's healthy  for you do consult a professional or  your doctor because this is always a  good idea to get a second view on the  decisions you make about your body but  it's kind of interesting that in the  modern westernized world we have an  abundance of food but actually most of  these food is nutrition poor so that is  to say that a lot of them lack  some certain vitamins that are super  important for our gut health and also  our brain health therefore and in an  austrian study one of the few vitamins  that was found to be lacking in a  majority of the population was vitamin b  folate magnesium and fiber and  especially fiber we now know is super  important for your gut health and fiber  can be found in a large bar of foods but  especially in fruits  and vegetables so as we all know this is  already super healthy for your health  but also for your guts then so i would  just recommend to eat an abundance of  fruit and veggies  and include them as much as possible in  your daily  eating regime so there has also been  this trend of including probiotics and  prebiotics in our diet  but  some of the findings that have been  found was for example that women who  were introduced to a fermented milk  product with probiotics  that their brain activity to certain  areas was modulated  by this  so probiotics can for example be found  in the form of pills in certain health  stores but the way i would introduce  these kind of  healthy  benefits of probiotics is true natural  foods so such as kefir kimchi sauerkraut  which is kind of popular here and of  course is super popular right now  kombucha which i think is super  delicious and for general brain health  there has been some science that  suggested that the mediterranean diet  is the most superior of all diets for  our  mental cognitive abilities so the  mediterranean diet consists of a few  things so it includes vegetables fruit  and nuts  legumes fish  unsaturated fatty acids such as found in  avocado  so but i would recommend instead of  running to the nearest supermarket and  buying all the italian  produce i would just look around in your  area to see what kind of things are  readily available that are for example  fruit and veggies or include some  natural  healthy fats so if you live in an area  that has an abundance of avocados of  course choose that but for example in  the netherlands that's not super readily  found so yeah i would just look in your  environment what's there go to your  local farmer migrants because i do think  our bodies are naturally kind of adapted  to the environment we live in so just  eating what is locally produced will  probably be most beneficial for your  body's physiological needs and your  mind's psychological needs so there's  also been some increased interest in  these adaptogenic herbs so adaptogenics  are these non-toxic herbs that you can  see here and they're mostly found in for  example health stores or in traditional  medicine they're used a lot and also  nowadays in more like lattes and teas  and there are some studies that show  that they are beneficial for example in  improving your gut health or reducing  stress  but to be very honest i don't find these  studies super convincing i do think the  benefits of these herbs are very minimal  but if you have some extra money or  interest or time to spare i would look  into some of the  benefits  so summing up i would recommend to eat  mainly like the mediterranean diet but  adapt it to your local produce  eat as much fruit and veg as possible  try to introduce some fermented foods  like kimchi or kombucha and if you're  super interested also sprinkle some  herbs on top stick as these can really  optimize  your guts as well as your brain  and then probably you're ready to live  that healthy god-brain life  so thank you for joining me on this  coffee time and until the next time  [Music]  you
 

